<!--
  Zenarmor (OPNsense) detection rules
  ID range: 130800-130899
  Decoder: etc/decoders/zenarmor-decoder.xml (program_name zenarmor, JSON after data=,
  plus srcip / dstip / dstport / zenarmor.index / zenarmor.domain / zenarmor.category).

  What Zenarmor sends
    One record per index: conn, dns, tls, http, sip and alert. A blocked threat
    arrives as SEVERAL records: a DNS block gives a dns record (the query, with
    qtype and the Recursion Desired bit is_RD), a conn record (the UDP flow to
    the DNS server) and an alert record; a blocked web connection gives conn +
    tls + alert. The alert record is Zenarmor's own block decision: there is
    exactly one per block, and alertinfo.match.dst_hostname holds the name it
    matched. Measured over 7 days on one site: 1,854 alert records, 1,811 dns and
    1,816 conn records for the same blocks. Alert records arrive later than the
    dns record of the same lookup: median 75 s, 90th percentile 16 min, maximum
    58 min.

  Grading
    * Not blocked is worse than blocked: an unblocked malicious connection was
      established (130801, level 12).
    * A blocked CONNECTION (web, any port but 53) is graded per event from its
      alert record (130802, 130804, 130820).
    * A blocked LOOKUP (port 53) never alerts by itself. Its alert record is
      recorded (130816, 130817, level 2); its dns record is classified and counted
      (1.5):
        resolver style  RD=0 (only a recursive resolver sends it) or qtype PTR,
                        NS or SOA: 130842 / 130843 / 130837 / 130838
        client style    RD=1 and another qtype: 130836 / 130839, unless the same
                        source sent a resolver-style record shortly before
                        (130844 / 130847 / 130848) or is listed (130818 / 130849)
      Client-style records are counted by 130832 (beaconing) and 130833 (many
      names); resolver-style records by 130834 and 130835. The dns record is used
      rather than the alert record because it carries RD and qtype and arrives
      together with the resolver's other lookups. Measured on the tuned site:
      1,584 of 1,811 dns records were resolver style on their own, and 220 of the
      other 227 (a domain controller forwarding A lookups) had a resolver-style
      record from the same host within 15 minutes before them.
      82% of the old 130802 alerts were one script name in cron mail
      ("<name>.sh" is a valid TLD) that a mail filter looked up.
    * A command-and-control name looked up by a client alerts at once from its
      dns record (130819, level 6).
    * Weak categories (Proxy, VPN, Parked) stay at level 5 or below, and at level 3
      when the policy blocked them (130828). Generic web-filtering blocks (Ads,
      Social ...) carry no security tag and stay at 130800, level 0.

  Correlation memory (read before tuning any timeframe)
    analysisd keeps correlation history only for the last memory_size events that
    matched a rule of level 1 or more (ossec.conf global memory_size, default
    8192; older events are freed and drop out of every if_matched list). On a
    manager processing about 75 events a second that is 15 to 20 minutes, on a
    busier one less. Every timeframe here is therefore 900 seconds or less, and
    the counts are per 15 minutes: a longer timeframe would silently mean "as far
    back as memory reaches".

  Read before changing anything (Wazuh 4.14.7, verified)
    * Sibling rules under one parent are evaluated in an unpredictable order
      (measured in 2026-08 on an earlier version: 130811 -> 130801 -> 130803
      -> 130802 for one parent; 130809-130813 were retired then and are not
      reused), and the first match ends the evaluation. Every sibling set here
      is mutually exclusive: by is_blocked, by index, by port (53 or not), by
      qtype, by RD, by CDB lookup (listed or not), or by a negative lookahead on
      the tag families; an override is a child of what it overrides. 1.3 had an
      overlap: "Botnet DGA Domains" matched both the malware family (Botnet) and
      the phishing family (DGA).
    * negate="yes" is unreliable. Every exclusion is a positive match: ^0$ / ^1$,
      a pcre2 lookahead, or lookup="not_address_match_key".
    * security_tags is an array; the field value is its JSON text, ["Phishing"].
      Do not anchor a tag regex with ^Phishing$.
    * same_field / different_field need dynamic fields (ip_src_saddr works,
      srcip does not). different_field compares each earlier event with the
      CURRENT one, so two names alternating also count: thresholds allow for it.
    * A frequency rule counts only events whose FINAL rule is the one it names
      or carries the group it names. An event whose final rule is the target of
      an if_matched_sid goes only to that sid list, not to group lists: that is
      why 130831 counts the group zenarmor_malware_conn and not sid 130802.
    * A rule listed under several parents (if_sid a,b) is registered once per
      parent for if_matched_group, so each event it ends is counted that many
      times (measured: frequency 3 fired on the second event). Every rule that
      carries a counted group has exactly one parent; that is why the malware
      and phishing families have their own copies (130815 / 130829 and so on).
    * if_matched_group only sees rules loaded BEFORE the composite rule: the
      evidence rules are above 130844 / 130847 / 130848 / 130850 on purpose.
    * A composite without frequency (130844, 130847, 130848, 130850) asks only
      whether one earlier event of the group exists in the timeframe and does
      not use it up.
    * A composite with if_matched_group and no if_sid becomes a child of every
      rule that carries the group, so it is a sibling of that rule's children.
    * Short words need \b: Tor matches Torrent otherwise.
-->
<group name="zenarmor,">

  <rule id="130800" level="0">
    <match>daemon=zenarmor</match>
    <description>Zenarmor event</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===== Malware, botnet, C2 (security_tags) ===== -->

  <!-- Not blocked: the connection was established. -->
  <rule id="130801" level="12">
    <if_sid>130800</if_sid>
    <field name="security_tags" type="pcre2">(?i)(Malware|Virus|Botnet|C&amp;C|Command.?and.?Control|Ransomware|Trojan|Spyware|\bWorm\b)</field>
    <field name="is_blocked">^0$</field>
    <description>[HIGH] Zenarmor: malicious connection was not blocked - $(security_tags) from: $(srcip) to: $(zenarmor.domain) $(dstip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,malware,c2,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Blocked. Since 1.4 this is the final rule only for Zenarmor's alert record of
       a blocked connection on a port other than 53; the other records and the
       lookups go to the children below. -->
  <rule id="130802" level="7">
    <if_sid>130800</if_sid>
    <field name="security_tags" type="pcre2">(?i)(Malware|Virus|Botnet|C&amp;C|Command.?and.?Control|Ransomware|Trojan|Spyware|\bWorm\b)</field>
    <field name="is_blocked">^1$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: malicious connection blocked - $(zenarmor.category) from: $(srcip) to: $(zenarmor.domain) $(dstip):$(dstport)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,malware,blocked,zenarmor_threat_block,zenarmor_malware_conn,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===== Phishing, fraud, spam, mining (security_tags) ===== -->
  <!-- The lookahead keeps an event with a malware tag in the malware family. -->

  <rule id="130803" level="10">
    <if_sid>130800</if_sid>
    <field name="security_tags" type="pcre2">(?i)^(?!.*(Malware|Virus|Botnet|C&amp;C|Command.?and.?Control|Ransomware|Trojan|Spyware|\bWorm\b)).*(Phishing|Crypto.?Mining|Scam|Fraud|Spam)</field>
    <field name="is_blocked">^0$</field>
    <description>[WARN] Zenarmor: phishing or fraud connection was not blocked - $(security_tags) from: $(srcip) to: $(zenarmor.domain) $(dstip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1566</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,phishing,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="130804" level="5">
    <if_sid>130800</if_sid>
    <field name="security_tags" type="pcre2">(?i)^(?!.*(Malware|Virus|Botnet|C&amp;C|Command.?and.?Control|Ransomware|Trojan|Spyware|\bWorm\b)).*(Phishing|Crypto.?Mining|Scam|Fraud|Spam)</field>
    <field name="is_blocked">^1$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: phishing or fraud connection blocked - $(zenarmor.category) from: $(srcip) to: $(zenarmor.domain) $(dstip):$(dstport)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1566</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,phishing,blocked,zenarmor_threat_block,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===== Blocked threats ===== -->

  <!-- Other records (malware family): conn, tls, http and dns copies of a block
       whose alert record reports it. -->
  <rule id="130815" level="2">
    <if_sid>130802</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index" type="pcre2">^(?!alert$)\w+$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: $(zenarmor.index) record of a blocked threat, reported by its alert record - from: $(srcip) to: $(zenarmor.domain)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,blocked,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- dns record, qtype PTR / NS / SOA: reverse lookups of mail or SSH peers and
       delegation lookups. Resolver style whatever the RD bit says. -->
  <rule id="130843" level="2">
    <if_sid>130815</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index">^dns$</field>
    <field name="qtype">^PTR$|^NS$|^SOA$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: blocked $(qtype) lookup, the source resolves names for a service - $(zenarmor.domain) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,blocked,dns,zenarmor_resolver_evidence,zenarmor_resolver_query,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- dns record of any other qtype: a client's lookup unless shown otherwise by
       the children below. DNS tunnels use TXT, CNAME, MX or NULL, so those stay
       here. -->
  <rule id="130836" level="2">
    <if_sid>130815</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index">^dns$</field>
    <field name="qtype" type="pcre2">^(?!(PTR|NS|SOA)$)\w+$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: blocked lookup - $(zenarmor.category) $(zenarmor.domain) $(qtype) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,blocked,dns,zenarmor_client_query,zenarmor_threat_block,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Recursion Desired = 0: an iterative query to an authoritative server. A stub
       resolver (every client, malware using the system or a public resolver) sets
       RD=1; only a recursive resolver sends RD=0. -->
  <rule id="130842" level="2">
    <if_sid>130836</if_sid>
    <field name="is_RD">^0$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: blocked iterative lookup, the source is a recursive resolver - $(zenarmor.domain) $(qtype) from: $(srcip) to: $(dstip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,blocked,dns,zenarmor_resolver_evidence,zenarmor_resolver_query,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- A forwarder that relays only A lookups looks like a client; the site can list it. -->
  <rule id="130818" level="2">
    <if_sid>130836</if_sid>
    <list field="srcip" lookup="address_match_key">etc/lists/jt-zenarmor-resolvers</list>
    <description>Zenarmor: blocked lookup forwarded by a listed resolver - $(zenarmor.category) $(zenarmor.domain) via: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,blocked,dns,zenarmor_resolver_query,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- A command-and-control name, looked up by a client: RD=1 (exclusive with
       130842) and not listed (exclusive with 130818). -->
  <rule id="130819" level="6">
    <if_sid>130836</if_sid>
    <field name="is_RD">^1$</field>
    <field name="security_tags" type="pcre2">(?i)(C&amp;C|Command.?and.?Control|\bC2\b|Ransomware|\bBotnet\b(?!\s*DGA))</field>
    <list field="srcip" lookup="not_address_match_key">etc/lists/jt-zenarmor-resolvers</list>
    <description>Zenarmor: lookup of a command-and-control domain blocked - $(zenarmor.category) $(zenarmor.domain) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,malware,c2,blocked,dns,zenarmor_client_query,zenarmor_threat_block,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Other records (phishing family): conn, tls, http and dns copies of a block
       whose alert record reports it. -->
  <rule id="130829" level="2">
    <if_sid>130804</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index" type="pcre2">^(?!alert$)\w+$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: $(zenarmor.index) record of a blocked threat, reported by its alert record - from: $(srcip) to: $(zenarmor.domain)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,blocked,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- dns record, qtype PTR / NS / SOA: reverse lookups of mail or SSH peers and
       delegation lookups. Resolver style whatever the RD bit says. -->
  <rule id="130838" level="2">
    <if_sid>130829</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index">^dns$</field>
    <field name="qtype">^PTR$|^NS$|^SOA$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: blocked $(qtype) lookup, the source resolves names for a service - $(zenarmor.domain) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,blocked,dns,zenarmor_resolver_evidence,zenarmor_resolver_query,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- dns record of any other qtype: a client's lookup unless shown otherwise by
       the children below. DNS tunnels use TXT, CNAME, MX or NULL, so those stay
       here. -->
  <rule id="130839" level="2">
    <if_sid>130829</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index">^dns$</field>
    <field name="qtype" type="pcre2">^(?!(PTR|NS|SOA)$)\w+$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: blocked lookup - $(zenarmor.category) $(zenarmor.domain) $(qtype) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,blocked,dns,zenarmor_client_query,zenarmor_threat_block,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Recursion Desired = 0: an iterative query to an authoritative server. A stub
       resolver (every client, malware using the system or a public resolver) sets
       RD=1; only a recursive resolver sends RD=0. -->
  <rule id="130837" level="2">
    <if_sid>130839</if_sid>
    <field name="is_RD">^0$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: blocked iterative lookup, the source is a recursive resolver - $(zenarmor.domain) $(qtype) from: $(srcip) to: $(dstip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,blocked,dns,zenarmor_resolver_evidence,zenarmor_resolver_query,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- A forwarder that relays only A lookups looks like a client; the site can list it. -->
  <rule id="130849" level="2">
    <if_sid>130839</if_sid>
    <list field="srcip" lookup="address_match_key">etc/lists/jt-zenarmor-resolvers</list>
    <description>Zenarmor: blocked lookup forwarded by a listed resolver - $(zenarmor.category) $(zenarmor.domain) via: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,blocked,dns,zenarmor_resolver_query,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Alert records of blocked lookups: the report of the block, recorded but not
       counted (the dns record of the same lookup is). -->
  <rule id="130816" level="2">
    <if_sid>130802</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index">^alert$</field>
    <field name="ip_dst_port">^53$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: lookup of a malicious domain blocked - $(zenarmor.category) $(zenarmor.domain) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,malware,blocked,dns,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="130817" level="2">
    <if_sid>130804</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index">^alert$</field>
    <field name="ip_dst_port">^53$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: lookup of a phishing or fraud domain blocked - $(zenarmor.category) $(zenarmor.domain) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,phishing,blocked,dns,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- A blocked connection to a command-and-control server: the host tried to reach
       its controller even though it was stopped. -->
  <rule id="130820" level="10">
    <if_sid>130802</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index">^alert$</field>
    <field name="ip_dst_port" type="pcre2">^(?!53$)\d+$</field>
    <field name="security_tags" type="pcre2">(?i)(C&amp;C|Command.?and.?Control|\bC2\b|Ransomware|\bBotnet\b(?!\s*DGA))</field>
    <description>[WARN] Zenarmor: connection to a command-and-control server blocked, check the host - $(zenarmor.category) from: $(srcip) to: $(zenarmor.domain) $(dstip):$(dstport)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,malware,c2,blocked,zenarmor_threat_block,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===== Unblocked lookups ===== -->

  <rule id="130821" level="8">
    <if_sid>130801</if_sid>
    <field name="ip_dst_port">^53$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: a malicious domain was resolved, not blocked - $(security_tags) $(zenarmor.domain) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,malware,dns,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="130822" level="6">
    <if_sid>130803</if_sid>
    <field name="ip_dst_port">^53$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: a phishing or fraud domain was resolved, not blocked - $(security_tags) $(zenarmor.domain) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,phishing,dns,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Resolved for a client behind a resolver: listed, RD=0, PTR/NS/SOA, or
       resolver evidence from the same source shortly before (130850). These are
       not counted by anything, so several parents are fine here. -->
  <rule id="130823" level="4">
    <if_sid>130821,130822</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index">^dns$</field>
    <list field="srcip" lookup="address_match_key">etc/lists/jt-zenarmor-resolvers</list>
    <description>Zenarmor: a listed resolver resolved a threat domain, not blocked, for a client behind it - $(security_tags) $(zenarmor.domain) via: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,dns,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="130845" level="4">
    <if_sid>130821,130822</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index">^dns$</field>
    <field name="is_RD">^0$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: a recursive resolver resolved a threat domain, not blocked, for a client behind it - $(security_tags) $(zenarmor.domain) via: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,dns,zenarmor_resolver_evidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="130846" level="4">
    <if_sid>130821,130822</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index">^dns$</field>
    <field name="qtype">^PTR$|^NS$|^SOA$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: a $(qtype) lookup of a threat domain was resolved, not blocked, by a host resolving names for a service - $(security_tags) $(zenarmor.domain) via: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,dns,zenarmor_resolver_evidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- The UDP flow record of the lookup: its dst_hostname is the DNS server. The
       dns record of the same lookup carries the name. -->
  <rule id="130824" level="2">
    <if_sid>130821,130822</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index">^conn$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: flow record of an unblocked threat lookup, reported by its dns record - from: $(srcip) to: $(dstip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,dns,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===== Resolver evidence from earlier records =====
       A client-style dns record (RD=1, A / AAAA / HTTPS ...) from a source that
       sent a resolver-style record (130842 / 130843 / 130837 / 130838 / 130845 /
       130846) within the last 15 minutes is a resolver forwarding a client's
       lookup. 900 s is the most the correlation memory holds on a busy manager
       (see the header); measured on the tuned site, 220 of 227 such records had
       evidence within 15 minutes and 219 within 5. One rule per parent so the
       counted group is registered once (header). -->
  <rule id="130844" level="2" timeframe="900">
    <if_sid>130836</if_sid>
    <if_matched_group>zenarmor_resolver_evidence</if_matched_group>
    <same_field>ip_src_saddr</same_field>
    <description>Zenarmor: blocked lookup forwarded by a resolver for a client behind it - $(zenarmor.category) $(zenarmor.domain) via: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,blocked,dns,zenarmor_resolver_query,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="130848" level="2" timeframe="900">
    <if_sid>130819</if_sid>
    <if_matched_group>zenarmor_resolver_evidence</if_matched_group>
    <same_field>ip_src_saddr</same_field>
    <description>Zenarmor: blocked command-and-control lookup forwarded by a resolver for a client behind it - $(zenarmor.category) $(zenarmor.domain) via: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,blocked,dns,zenarmor_resolver_query,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="130847" level="2" timeframe="900">
    <if_sid>130839</if_sid>
    <if_matched_group>zenarmor_resolver_evidence</if_matched_group>
    <same_field>ip_src_saddr</same_field>
    <description>Zenarmor: blocked lookup forwarded by a resolver for a client behind it - $(zenarmor.category) $(zenarmor.domain) via: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,blocked,dns,zenarmor_resolver_query,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="130850" level="4" timeframe="900">
    <if_sid>130821,130822</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index">^dns$</field>
    <if_matched_group>zenarmor_resolver_evidence</if_matched_group>
    <same_field>ip_src_saddr</same_field>
    <description>Zenarmor: a resolver resolved a threat domain, not blocked, for a client behind it - $(security_tags) $(zenarmor.domain) via: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,dns,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===== Proxy, anonymizer, VPN ===== -->

  <rule id="130805" level="5">
    <if_sid>130800</if_sid>
    <field name="security_tags" type="pcre2">(?i)^(?!.*(Malware|Virus|Botnet|C&amp;C|Command.?and.?Control|Ransomware|Trojan|Spyware|\bWorm\b|Phishing|Crypto.?Mining|Scam|Fraud|Spam)).*(Hacking|Proxy|Anonymizer|\bTor\b|\bVPN\b|Compromised|Suspicious)</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: suspicious or evasive connection - $(security_tags) from: $(srcip) to: $(zenarmor.domain) $(dstip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1090</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,evasion,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- category is the web category of a tls record. In practice a record has either
       security_tags or a category, not both. -->
  <rule id="130806" level="5">
    <if_sid>130800</if_sid>
    <field name="category" type="pcre2">(?i)(Hacking|Proxy|Anonymizer|\bTor\b|\bVPN\b)</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: connection in an evasion category - $(category) from: $(srcip) to: $(zenarmor.domain) $(dstip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1090</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,evasion,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- An evasion category that the policy blocked: the policy did its job, so this
       is information, below the blocked phishing (5) and malware (7) levels. The
       three quiet rules below are children of this one as well, so a blocked relay
       or allowlisted name still ends at level 2 whichever sibling is tried first. -->
  <rule id="130828" level="3">
    <if_sid>130805,130806</if_sid>
    <field name="is_blocked">^1$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: evasion-category connection blocked by policy - $(zenarmor.category) from: $(srcip) to: $(zenarmor.domain)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,evasion,blocked,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- iCloud Private Relay is built into Apple devices with iCloud+, and Zenarmor
       files its endpoints under Proxy: 629 of 978 evasion alerts in 7 days. A site
       that forbids it should block mask.icloud.com and mask-h2.icloud.com in DNS,
       which is Apple's documented way to turn it off for a network. -->
  <rule id="130825" level="2">
    <if_sid>130805,130806,130828</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.domain" type="pcre2">(?i)^mask(-[a-z0-9]+)?\.icloud\.com$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: Apple iCloud Private Relay connection - from: $(srcip) to: $(zenarmor.domain)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,evasion,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- check.torproject.org is a web page and the source of the Tor exit list that
       threat-intelligence tools download (measured: two log servers, once an hour
       each, 349 of 978 evasion alerts). Using Tor itself goes to relays, not here. -->
  <rule id="130826" level="2">
    <if_sid>130805,130806,130828</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.domain" type="pcre2">(?i)^check\.torproject\.org$</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: Tor Project check page or exit list download - from: $(srcip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,evasion,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- The site's own allowlist for the weak categories only (a corporate VPN
       endpoint filed under VPN, for example). It never silences malware or phishing. -->
  <rule id="130827" level="2">
    <if_sid>130805,130806,130828</if_sid>
    <list field="zenarmor.domain" lookup="match_key">etc/lists/jt-zenarmor-domain-allow</list>
    <description>Zenarmor: evasion-category connection to an allowlisted domain - from: $(srcip) to: $(zenarmor.domain)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,evasion,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===== Parked domains: recorded at level 0 ===== -->
  <!-- Measured 2026-08-30: very high false-positive rate. The classifier appears to
       key on the apex having no A record, which also describes CDN-fronted
       application backends (one client produced about 2,900 events a day). Real DGA
       activity carries Zenarmor's Botnet DGA Domains tag instead. -->
  <rule id="130807" level="0">
    <if_sid>130800</if_sid>
    <field name="security_tags" type="pcre2">(?i)^(?!.*(Malware|Virus|Botnet|C&amp;C|Command.?and.?Control|Ransomware|Trojan|Spyware|\bWorm\b|Phishing|Crypto.?Mining|Scam|Fraud|Spam|Hacking|Proxy|Anonymizer|\bTor\b|\bVPN\b|Compromised|Suspicious)).*Parked</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: connection to a parked domain - from: $(srcip) to: $(zenarmor.domain) $(dstip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,parked,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="130808" level="0">
    <if_sid>130800</if_sid>
    <field name="category" type="pcre2">(?i)Parked</field>
    <description>Zenarmor: connection to a parked domain - from: $(srcip) to: $(zenarmor.domain) $(dstip)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,parked,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===== Correlation (counts per 15 minutes, see the header) ===== -->

  <!-- Unblocked malicious connections, one host. Several records per connection
       (conn, tls) are counted, so 6 is about two or three connections. -->
  <rule id="130830" level="13" frequency="6" timeframe="300">
    <if_matched_sid>130801</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>ip_src_saddr</same_field>
    <description>[HIGH] Zenarmor: one internal host made repeated unblocked malicious connections in a short window, the host may be compromised: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,malware,c2,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Blocked malicious connections, one host, sustained. One page view opens four
       to six connections at once (measured: 17 alert records in four minutes from
       one visit), so 20 in 15 minutes asks for more than one page view. -->
  <rule id="130831" level="10" frequency="20" timeframe="900">
    <if_matched_group>zenarmor_malware_conn</if_matched_group>
    <same_field>ip_src_saddr</same_field>
    <description>[WARN] Zenarmor: one internal host keeps retrying malicious connections, all blocked so far, check the host: $(srcip) last: $(zenarmor.domain)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,malware,blocked,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- The same blocked threat name looked up again and again by a client: 15 dns
       records in 15 minutes. A browser asks A, AAAA and HTTPS for one name, so one
       visit is three records; 15 is a program asking about once a minute. A slower
       beacon outlasts the correlation memory and is not detected here. -->
  <rule id="130832" level="10" frequency="15" timeframe="900">
    <if_matched_group>zenarmor_client_query</if_matched_group>
    <same_field>ip_src_saddr</same_field>
    <same_field>zenarmor.domain</same_field>
    <description>[WARN] Zenarmor: one host keeps looking up the same blocked threat domain, a sign of malware beaconing: $(srcip) $(zenarmor.domain) (a forwarding DNS server can be listed in jt-zenarmor-resolvers)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,malware,c2,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Many different blocked threat names from one client within 15 minutes
       (client-style lookups and blocked connections): DGA malware or a host with
       several infections. -->
  <rule id="130833" level="10" frequency="20" timeframe="900">
    <if_matched_group>zenarmor_threat_block</if_matched_group>
    <same_field>ip_src_saddr</same_field>
    <different_field>zenarmor.domain</different_field>
    <description>[WARN] Zenarmor: one host was blocked from many different threat domains within 15 minutes, possible infection or DGA: $(srcip) last: $(zenarmor.domain) (a forwarding DNS server can be listed in jt-zenarmor-resolvers)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1568.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,malware,c2,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- A resolver asking for the same blocked threat name 40 times in 15 minutes:
       some client behind it is probably beaconing; the resolver's query log names
       it. A resolver sends one lookup to several servers (measured: 4 to 6 dns
       records per lookup, peak 12 in 15 minutes). -->
  <rule id="130834" level="6" frequency="40" timeframe="900">
    <if_matched_group>zenarmor_resolver_query</if_matched_group>
    <same_field>ip_src_saddr</same_field>
    <same_field>zenarmor.domain</same_field>
    <description>Zenarmor: a resolver keeps looking up the same blocked threat domain for a client behind it, check its query log: $(zenarmor.domain) via: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,malware,c2,dns,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Many different blocked threat names through one resolver within 15 minutes:
       a client behind it may run DGA malware (a spam run being filtered looks the
       same without the resolver's query log). Measured peak of the different_field
       count on the tuned site: 13. -->
  <rule id="130835" level="7" frequency="40" timeframe="900">
    <if_matched_group>zenarmor_resolver_query</if_matched_group>
    <same_field>ip_src_saddr</same_field>
    <different_field>zenarmor.domain</different_field>
    <description>Zenarmor: a resolver looked up many different blocked threat domains within 15 minutes for clients behind it, check its query log: via: $(srcip) last: $(zenarmor.domain)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1568.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,malware,c2,dns,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===== Out-of-band testing domains ===== -->
  <!-- interactsh, Burp Collaborator, canarytokens and similar services hand out a
       unique name per payload: a lookup means some host processed an injected
       payload (log4j-style lookups, SSRF, a mail header) or opened a canary. Through
       a resolver it is still meaningful: a host behind it did the processing.
       130840 is also a child of 130831 and 130833, which are siblings of 130816
       under 130802, so a burst that trips one of them still reports the testing
       domain; that is why this block comes last (an if_sid must name a rule
       loaded before it, or it is dropped with only a warning).
       Measured: a mail gateway resolved one interactsh name 13 times within four
       minutes, which Zenarmor tagged Malware/Virus. -->
  <rule id="130840" level="10">
    <if_sid>130816,130817,130831,130833</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index">^alert$</field>
    <field name="zenarmor.domain" type="pcre2">(?i)(^|\.)(oast\.(pro|live|site|online|fun|me)|interact\.sh|interactsh\.com|oastify\.com|burpcollaborator\.net|canarytokens\.com|dnslog\.cn|ceye\.io)$</field>
    <description>[WARN] Zenarmor: out-of-band testing domain looked up (blocked), a host processed an injected payload: $(zenarmor.domain) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,oast,blocked,dns,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="130841" level="10">
    <if_sid>130821,130822,130823,130845,130846,130850</if_sid>
    <field name="zenarmor.index">^dns$</field>
    <field name="zenarmor.domain" type="pcre2">(?i)(^|\.)(oast\.(pro|live|site|online|fun|me)|interact\.sh|interactsh\.com|oastify\.com|burpcollaborator\.net|canarytokens\.com|dnslog\.cn|ceye\.io)$</field>
    <description>[WARN] Zenarmor: out-of-band testing domain resolved (not blocked), a host processed an injected payload: $(zenarmor.domain) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>zenarmor,oast,dns,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
